 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Tender, fairly lean cuts work best: sirloin (antrikot), ribeye (kontrfile) or top round.</li><li>Avoid stewing cuts; they are too tough for quick cooking.</li><li>Ask the butcher to slice it on the machine at <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"len\" data-u=\"mm\" data-a=\"2\" data-b=\"3\" data-s=\"0\">2–3 mm</span> if they will.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Which beef to buy"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Asian pear (nashi) has an enzyme that softens the meat and adds fruitiness.</li><li>A crisp apple works too.</li><li>Kiwi is much stronger: use only <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tbsp\" data-a=\"1\" data-s=\"0\">1 tbsp</span> and marinate no longer than 1 hour.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Pear is the tenderiser"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Marinated bulgogi freezes well flat in a bag for up to 2 months.</li><li>Thaw it in the fridge overnight and cook as above.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Freezing ahead"
  }
 ],
